1. Successful signals: Strongest negative scores (inhibitory effects on IFNG production) cluster heavily in T-cell receptor signaling (ZAP70, VAV1, ITK, CD3E, CD28, LCK) and core transcription factors driving T-helper differentiation (TBX21, STAT3, RELA). Positive scores (repressors of IFNG) highlight negative regulators of immune activation and mTOR (TSC1, TSC2, SOCS3, CISH).

2. Weak signals: Scores close to zero or with modest magnitudes involve broader metabolic regulators, general kinases, and transcription factors not centrally wired into proximal lymphocyte activation or cytokine receptor feedback loops (e.g., IL1B, NFKB1, TYK2, CASP1). 

3. Working hypothesis: Productive signal originates primarily from the proximal T-cell receptor (TCR) signaling cascade, downstream costimulatory pathways, and specific negative feedback loops (such as suppressors of cytokine signaling and mTOR pathway components). Perturbing components that tune lymphocyte activation states yields the highest magnitude changes in IFNG production.

4. Next-round principle: Prioritize exploitation over exploration by focusing heavily on immediate interactors, paralogs, and direct downstream effectors of the validated TCR signaling and negative regulatory networks. Limit broad random screening in favor of deep neighborhood expansion around the strongest negative and positive hits identified so far.
